00:09:45.778 --> 00:09:52.165
<v Daniel>other systems, as the very last thing in the context window, it stopped the bad behavior 90% of the time.</v>

106
00:09:52.305 --> 00:09:54.027
<v Maya>Okay, that sounds like a good success rate.</v>

107
00:09:54.388 --> 00:10:02.875
<v Daniel>But if they place that exact same reminder just three turns earlier in the conversation history, it only stopped the behavior 40% of the time.</v>

108
00:10:03.075 --> 00:10:07.459
<v Maya>Wait, a drop from 90 to 40 just because the prompt was three turns older?</v>

109
00:10:07.699 --> 00:10:08.520
<v Daniel>Just three turns.</v>

110
00:10:09.028 --> 00:10:12.190
<v Maya>Why does a few lines of text make such a massive difference?</v>

111
00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:27.180
<v Daniel>It comes down to how these models generate text, which is autoregressive. They predict the next word based on all the previous words. They also use attention mechanisms, which decide which parts of the context window are most important at any given millisecond.</v>

112
00:10:28.421 --> 00:10:42.920
<v Daniel>When the model generates a few turns of its own activity, especially activity focused on hacking or problem solving, The mathematical weight of its own recent text essentially drowns out the older safety instruction. Oh, wow. The momentum of its own malicious</v>

113
00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:50.345
<v Daniel>generation literally overrides the rules it was given. And they saw this same pattern across the other incidents as well.</v>

114
00:10:50.485 --> 00:10:55.168
<v Maya>There is also this detail from the follow-up questioning that is just incredibly unsettling to me.</v>

115
00:10:55.348 --> 00:10:56.569
